  "slug": "scientific-fair-5429",
  "summary": "New tools model inheritance, bursting and RNA decay from imaging and metabolic-labeling data, while normalization methods address batch and background noise.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of explicit statistical or dynamical models to extract mechanistic parameters from sparse or noisy measurements. PRNODE fits ODE-based mother and daughter protein regulatory networks with a fused group-lasso to quantify inheritance in yeast lineages. buRst infers burst frequency, burst size and RNA degradation from scSLAM-seq during acute perturbations, and RNAdecayCafe provides uniformly processed half-life estimates from SLAM-seq and TimeLapse-seq across 11 human cell lines. Supporting methods address measurement quality and resolution: mapfx.norm removes background noise and batch effects in cytometry, BASIN uses IgG-derived background anchors for CUT&RUN/CUT&Tag normalization, RecurSieve detects subtle distributional scRNA-seq differences, and Bulk2SC generates single-cell profiles from bulk data with a Gaussian mixture VAE.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
